<h2>Composition, Composite or Asphalt: What’s the Difference?</h2>
<p>The names get used interchangeably, which confuses a lot of homeowners. "Composition shingles" and "asphalt shingles" usually mean the same thing: a fiberglass mat coated in asphalt and topped with ceramic-coated mineral granules. The word composition simply refers to that layered build. When people search for an Austin composite roofing contractor, they are usually asking about the same product, although "composite" is also used for synthetic shingles made from polymers or recycled materials that imitate slate or shake.</p>
<p>On this page we focus on composition (asphalt) shingles, which make up the vast majority of residential roofs in the area. If you are weighing shingles against a longer-lasting system, see our <a href="/services/metal-roofing/">metal roofing</a> page.</p>

