<h2>Class 4 Impact-Resistant Shingles for Austin Hail</h2>
<p>Hail is the number-one reason roofs in Central Texas are replaced early. Class 4 shingles have passed the most demanding level of the UL 2218 impact test, in which a 2-inch steel ball is dropped onto the same spot without cracking the shingle. They are built with tougher mats or polymer-modified asphalt that flexes instead of fracturing.</p>
<div class="pros-cons">
  <div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul>
    <li>Better resistance to bruising and cracking from common hail sizes</li>
    <li>Many Texas insurers offer premium discounts for impact-rated roofs — ask your agent</li>
    <li>Look identical to standard architectural shingles</li>
    <li>Often carry higher wind ratings as well</li>
  </ul></div>
  <div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul>
    <li>Cost more than standard architectural shingles</li>
    <li>Impact-resistant does not mean hail-proof — very large hail can still cause damage</li>
    <li>Discount amounts and eligibility vary by insurer and policy</li>
  </ul></div>
</div>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><p><strong>Before you buy:</strong> call your insurance agent and ask whether they offer a discount for a Class 4 roof and what documentation they need. We can provide the product details and installation paperwork your insurer may request.</p></div>

<h2>Installation Details That Decide How Long Shingles Last</h2>
<p>Two roofs built with the same shingle can last very different lengths of time. The difference is almost always installation. On every composition roof, Austin Roofing pays attention to:</p>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><h3>Nail placement</h3><p>Nails driven in the manufacturer’s nailing zone, flush and not overdriven, using the high-wind pattern. High or crooked nails are a leading cause of blow-offs.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Starter strips at eaves and rakes</h3><p>Dedicated starter shingles with a sealant line keep the first course from lifting in a gust.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Underlayment and valleys</h3><p>Synthetic underlayment over the deck and self-adhered membrane in valleys, around chimneys and at every penetration.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Ventilation</h3><p>Balanced soffit intake and ridge exhaust. A hot, stagnant attic shortens shingle life and can affect warranty coverage.</p></li>
  <li><h3>Proper sealing</h3><p>Shingle seal strips activate with sun and heat. In cool months, or on shaded slopes, we hand-seal where the manufacturer calls for it.</p></li>
</ol>

<h2>Choosing Composition Shingles for Austin Homes</h2>
<p>A few local factors are worth weighing when you choose Austin composition roofing:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Color and heat.</strong> Lighter colors and shingles with solar-reflective granules can lower attic temperatures during long Austin summers. Darker colors can look sharp but absorb more heat.</li>
  <li><strong>HOA rules.</strong> Many subdivisions in Round Rock, Cedar Park, Pflugerville and Georgetown restrict shingle colors or require a specific profile. We bring samples and product sheets for your HOA submittal.</li>
  <li><strong>Hail exposure.</strong> If your neighborhood has seen repeated hail claims, the extra cost of a Class 4 shingle often pays back through fewer repairs and potential insurance savings.</li>
  <li><strong>Trees.</strong> On lots with heavy oak or cedar cover, shingles stay damp longer and collect debris. Algae-resistant granules and clean valleys help.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Signs Your Composition Roof Needs Attention</h2>
<p>Composition shingles age gradually, and in Central Texas the warning signs usually show up on the south- and west-facing slopes first, where the afternoon sun is strongest. Watch for:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
  <li><strong>Granules in the gutters.</strong> A little after a new install is normal; handfuls years later mean the asphalt is losing its UV shield.</li>
  <li><strong>Curling or cupping.</strong> Edges turning up or the centers sinking are classic signs of heat aging and poor attic ventilation.</li>
  <li><strong>Cracked or brittle tabs.</strong> Shingles that snap instead of flex will break loose in the next windstorm.</li>
  <li><strong>Dark, circular bruises.</strong> Soft spots where granules are missing are typical hail damage, even when nothing is leaking yet.</li>
  <li><strong>Exposed nail heads or lifted rows.</strong> These suggest the seal strips have failed or the original nailing was off.</li>
  <li><strong>Dark streaks.</strong> Usually algae rather than damage, but on shaded, tree-covered slopes it can signal a roof that stays damp too long.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are seeing two or three of these at once, a free <a href="/services/roof-inspection/">roof inspection</a> will tell you whether repairs will buy you meaningful time or whether it is time to start planning a new roof.</p>

<h2>Composition Roof Repair and Replacement</h2>
<p>Not every worn shingle roof needs replacing. Missing tabs, lifted shingles, cracked pipe boots and flashing problems can usually be repaired by our <a href="/services/roof-repair/">roof repair</a> team, using matched shingles where they are still available. When a roof is losing granules across every slope, curling, or has widespread hail bruising, a full <a href="/services/roof-replacement/">Austin roof replacement</a> is the better investment. After a hailstorm, start with a free inspection; we document the damage and can meet your adjuster. See our <a href="/services/hail-damage-repair/">hail damage repair</a> page for how that works.</p>
